  "result": "I would close the distance gap in human perception\u2014making the consequences of an action felt just as vividly when they happen to a stranger ten thousand miles away, or a century from now, as when they happen to someone standing right in front of you.\n\nMost of the world\u2019s worst failures don't seem to come from pure malice or a lack of intelligence. In small rooms, humans are remarkably cooperative, inventive, and compassionate. When someone falls in front of you, the instinct to reach out is almost automatic. \n\nThe tragedy is that human moral intuition evolved for small tribes and immediate threats, so it drops off sharply with distance and time. It becomes easy to ignore suffering that is abstract, delayed, or hidden behind supply chains, borders, and statistics. If people instinctively felt the real weight of distant and future consequences the way they feel the heat of a fire right in front of them, most of the machinery of war, exploitation, and ecological neglect would simply stop making sense to build.",
